# Round 2 (objective steward#7) — added; sections 1–4 above stand unchanged

## R1. What the objective asks (issue steward#7, quoted)
- "Use gemini-3.8-flash; if it is refused, an available Gemini flash model, recorded with the reason."
- "1. Shot descriptions for Caminandes 3: draft the description of every shot of the film with CrewCut's own describe step, and record the model that drafted them."
- "2. Complete c for the evaluation run of the previous round (the fixed pipeline on caminandes3-L1-0000-scr, smoke profile): shot correspondence judged by Gemini through CrewCut's continuity judge from those descriptions, together with the cut F1 already measured, and every other component of c the code computes for that run. The film has no dialogue, so WER and line-onset error do not apply: record them as not applicable. A component that cannot be computed is marked "not run" with the reason, without a conclusion."
- "3. Record the results and the spend: the requests made to Gemini, by purpose, with the token usage the responses report, and the cost where a price is known (CrewCut's `configs/prices.yaml` or the provider's published price, with its source and date)."
- "The real generators (T0) are still out of this round: CrewCut's http back end does not match Gemini, and whether to write an adapter is CrewCut's maintainer's decision. ... do not change CrewCut's fixed configuration or protocol, record the decisions Issue #3 leaves open without choosing, and nothing goes back to CrewCut."
- Deliver: "the descriptions with their model, the completed c with each component and how it was computed, the spend, and an updated statement of what was not run."
- Budget: 30 turns, 1800 model seconds, 1.5 h wall clock; at most 300 requests to Gemini (shared by all positions).

## R2. Protocol items relevant to round 2 (sources as in section 2)
- Components of Stage 2 "Continuity" (cfg-independent; `crewcut/scoring/continuity.py` docstring @df54b3a): cut alignment F1 (0.5 s and 1.0 s); shot correspondence = duration-weighted share of original shots whose description a judge accepts for the remake's frames in the same interval (judge never sees the original's pixels); dialogue (WER, median line-onset error); character continuity (cluster purity, speaker and visual embeddings); scene order (1 − normalised edit distance of scene sequences).
- Judge frames: `continuity.judge_frames_per_second` 1, `judge_frames_min` 2, `judge_frames_max` 8 (cfg). Cut tolerances `[0.5, 1.0]`.
- Descriptions: format "[shot size] of [subject] [action], [camera move]" (Issue #3 §1.3), drafted by the VLM (`crewcut/build/describe.py`, env `CREWCUT_VLM_*`); judge `crewcut/scoring/judge.py`, env `CREWCUT_JUDGE_*`.
- Prices: `configs/prices.yaml` has only `default` {input 5.00, output 25.00, cache_read 0.50, cache_write 6.25} USD per million tokens; a model absent is billed at `default` with `detail.price_missing = true`.
- Wording that the code's docstring gives and the documents do not turn into a formula: how the components combine into the single "c" is not stated in the documents read (README: R=(Q, Q·c, Q·S, $)). Position that computes c must quote the code for it; if the code has no combination, report components separately and record the gap.

## R3. Maintainer's decisions (still open, untouched)
All of section 3, plus: the VLM that drafts descriptions (Issue #3 open item 1) and the judge model (item 2) are the maintainer's; this round's use of gemini-3.8-flash is the objective's instruction, not a decision of the maintainer, and results made with it must say so. Model prices: no row for any Gemini model; a provider price used is recorded with source and date and is not a CrewCut price. Adapter for the http back end/real generators: maintainer's. New gap G7: the combination of components into c (above). New gap G8: documents do not say whether descriptions are to be human-corrected before judging (Issue #3 lists a human round as the maintainer's).

## R4. Positions
See `assignment.json`. Gemini calls are shared (300 total): scorer alone calls Gemini (describe and judge) and keeps the spend ledger in scores/; task-maker publishes the descriptions; executor keeps the not-run statement; auditor checks counts against the limit. Each caller logs every request's purpose, model, usage.
